WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.177 --> 00:00:01.260
SAAD ALI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.050
My name is Saad Ali.

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:03.425
I'm a software
engineer at Google

00:00:03.425 --> 00:00:06.140
and I'm here at
KubeCon Greg Castle.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.550
Greg is the tech lead of
the security team at GKE.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:11.970
Greg, you have a huge mandate.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.630
Can you talk a little bit
about what you work on?

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:15.380
GREG CASTLE: Yeah.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.080
So my team handles the
security of a bunch

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.390
of parts of the GKE system.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.480
We look after security
of masters and kind

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.790
of security
configurations of masters

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.730
as well as helping out
with other teams that look

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:31.100
after various other subsystems.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.320
So we interact with
storage, we interact

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.210
with the node team,
networking, a bunch of others,

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:38.990
and kind of help drive
security direction

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:40.280
across the whole product.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.250
As well as in the
open source, we

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:46.100
have a number of
people on SIG-AUTH.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.580
So we have a couple of the
leaders in that community,

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.510
and we have people on the
Kubernetes product security

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:57.440
team that get all the incoming
security vulnerabilities

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.700
for Kubernetes, and triage
them, and respond to them,

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.280
so yeah, a lot of
stuff internally

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.210
in protecting GKE clusters,
and also externally protecting

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:11.047
Kubernetes clusters in general.

00:01:11.047 --> 00:01:13.130
SAAD ALI: That is a lot
of stuff that you work on.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.713
Let's focus in a little bit on
the open source side of things.

00:01:15.713 --> 00:01:17.330
Where are things as
they stand today,

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.150
and where they
going in the future?

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.430
GREG CASTLE: So I think
we've made a lot of progress

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.130
recently with--

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:28.190
some interesting areas that
have seen a fair bit of focus

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:30.750
in 2018 have been
service accounts.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.580
So Mike Danese and I are
talking about this on Thursday.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.260
You can come to the Container
Identity Working Group

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.880
deep dive, where we're going
to talk a fair bit about how

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.750
we had these older service
accounts that Kubernetes first

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:47.450
started with that had a
number of security problems

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:48.390
that were--

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.160
have been addressing in 2018 and
moving to kind of a new model.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.280
And the differences
are mainly that those--

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.200
that first generation
of service accounts

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.485
were basically static keys
that lived in the the secrets

00:02:00.485 --> 00:02:01.430
database.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.380
They lived forever.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:04.950
It was very difficult
to rotate them,

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:10.759
so we've had some security
incidents where a key--

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:13.370
with various, like subsystems
of Kubernetes, where

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.530
a key was leaked in some way.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:19.525
And then we need to move onto a
new Kubernetes service account

00:02:19.525 --> 00:02:20.900
and revoke those
old credentials,

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.960
and it's all very painful
in the current version

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.310
of service accounts.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:29.000
And so we've developed a bunch
of APIs and some new ways

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.620
to mount in these new
Kubernetes service account

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.135
secrets that no longer involve
that centralized database.

00:02:38.135 --> 00:02:42.420
They can be like kind of scoped
and sort of bound to a Pod.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.150
So a lot of things get easier.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.310
So they're limited
lifetime, the kubelet

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:49.230
will handle rotation of
them automatically for you.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:52.880
The client-go Kubernetes
libraries have been updated

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.410
so that the users--

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.920
the consumers of
those service account

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.180
tokens don't have to
worry too much about it as

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.510
long as they're using
a client library.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:05.750
And they can be audience-bound,
which is a new thing too.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:09.120
So previously, there wasn't
any audience binding,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.210
which meant those tokens
weren't particularly

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:13.370
useful outside of--
or they weren't safely

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.110
useful outside of the cluster.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.390
And now we can say--

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:19.340
you can go to the
Kubernetes master

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.040
and say, give me a
credential that I

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.960
want to go use with
HashiCorp Vault,

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.520
or I want to go authenticate
some other system.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.610
And you can say, this
token should only

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.980
be used with that
destination system.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.836
And that destination
system wouldn't then

00:03:32.836 --> 00:03:35.210
be able to turn around and
take it back to the API server

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:37.850
and have some control
over the API server

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.710
that that service account has.

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:41.960
So there's quite a bit of--
there's been a lot of work

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.790
in that in 2018.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:45.410
And Mike, in particular--

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:48.060
Mike Danese hae-- led a lot
of that in the community.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.670
And so we're just now
getting to a point--

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.370
so some of those
APIs are in beta.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.310
One of them is in alpha in 1.13,
but we're getting to a point

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:57.620
where we need people
to start using them

00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.540
and get feedback on them.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.580
So if that's
interesting, I definitely

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.940
look at the Container Identity
Working Group deep dive

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:05.840
session.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.941
I will talk about that
in some more detail.

00:04:07.941 --> 00:04:10.190
SAAD ALI: That sounds like
a lot of awesome work going

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:11.470
on the open source
side of things.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:13.470
So I think folks are going
to be looking forward

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.579
to seeing service account
rotations becoming something

00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:17.879
that they can start using.

00:04:17.879 --> 00:04:19.670
Can you talk a little
bit more about what's

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:21.860
going on the GKE side
of things in terms

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.900
of keeping customers secure?

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:24.650
GREG CASTLE: Yeah.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:29.450
So that service account work is
playing into a larger identity

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:30.750
effort at Google.

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:35.720
So we are-- we know there's
some kind of usability

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:39.650
pain and some security things
that we'd like to address

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:41.720
with accessing Google services.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.850
So getting access to
Google credentials

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.490
to access Google services
is currently more painful

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:47.610
than it should be.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.540
And so this work we're
doing in the community

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:52.760
is kind of foundational
for that work

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:55.860
to improve that identity
integration with Google.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.560
And so there's active
work in that area.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.430
We're not quite ready
to announce it yet,

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.820
but if you are a GKE customer,
definitely talk to us about it,

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.890
and we can get you involved
early in that process.

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:10.240
And so we'll be improving
the security of the identity

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:13.790
integration with Google, and the
usability of it as well, too.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:16.360
And I think that's going to be
quite powerful and flexible.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.590
So that's been a big
focus for our team.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:23.120
There's some other efforts on
the way under like a general,

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:24.700
I'd say, hardening bucket.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:30.400
So we're both in Kubernetes
and on the Google side.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.980
So on the Kubernetes side,
we've added the ability

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:36.666
for the kubelet to be
bootstrapped from what's

00:05:36.666 --> 00:05:38.290
called a trusted
platform module, which

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:42.340
is a kind of trusted,
cryptographic device that

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:45.620
can be used as a root
of trust for things.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.390
And so we want to be able to
have a strong cryptographic way

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:53.320
to bootstrap nodes that's better
than like some sort of shared

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:56.230
key that we give
to nodes to say,

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:59.170
this is a thing that should
be able to join the cluster.

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:02.530
Ideally, we have something
like a TPM that can say,

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:05.300
here's a cryptographic
assertion that says who I am.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.024
And then write rules
about who should

00:06:07.024 --> 00:06:08.190
be able to join the cluster.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:11.230
And so we've added that
functionality into Kubernetes,

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:14.230
and we're working on kind of
interesting Google side stuff

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:15.880
for that too.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:19.120
So no announcements there
yet either, but there's

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:20.765
more interesting stuff coming.

00:06:20.765 --> 00:06:21.390
SAAD ALI: Cool.

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:23.560
So it sounds like a lot
of new, cool functionality

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.590
to make Kubernetes
more secure on GKE.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:29.920
Can you talk more about
the bug bounty program,

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:32.020
and what we do to
discover vulnerabilities,

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:34.161
and how we deal with them?

00:06:34.161 --> 00:06:36.160
GREG CASTLE: Yeah, so on
a few different fronts,

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.830
Google has its own bug
bounty program right now.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:42.610
So you can-- if you find a
security vulnerability in GKE

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:45.640
or in, really, any Google
product within the scope

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:48.730
of the bug bounty and GKE is
in scope for the bug bounty,

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:50.800
then we will pay you money.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:57.070
So please try out, test the
bounds of the GKE security.

00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:58.990
And we're happy to
receive those and we're

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:01.900
happy to pay for
those vulnerabilities.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:05.920
Similarly, the CNCF is also
standing up its own bug bounty

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:08.710
program, and that's
currently in process now.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:13.570
So we're working on setting
up the structure for that

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:16.450
to be able to handle those
vulnerabilities coming

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:18.230
in from a program like that.

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:21.830
And that will also set
Kubernetes up in a good place

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:22.330
too.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:25.255
And we're also looking at some--

00:07:25.255 --> 00:07:27.880
for Kubernetes and
the CNCF is also

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.490
kind of looking at some threat
modeling and that kind of work

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:31.960
too.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:35.290
So yeah, the bug bounty
program, I think,

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:39.070
is a symbol of an organization
being mature enough

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:41.900
to know that security
bugs do happen.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:45.610
And if we can force multiply by
taking advantage of that great

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:48.785
security researcher
community and paying for them

00:07:48.785 --> 00:07:51.160
for their effort-- it is a
lot of effort to find security

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:53.320
vulnerabilities in
a lot of cases--

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:56.890
then I think it's a real symbol
of maturity and something

00:07:56.890 --> 00:08:01.155
that we'd love to see replicated
as widely as possible.

00:08:01.155 --> 00:08:01.780
SAAD ALI: Cool.

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:03.550
And I understand we
work very closely

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:07.460
with partners to make sure that
our entire ecosystem is safe.

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:10.140
Can you introduce Shopify?

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:12.070
GREG CASTLE: Yeah.

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:14.440
So Shane, do you
want to come over?

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:17.470
How do you-- join
us on camera here.

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:21.670
So Shane and I are
presenting on Thursday.

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:23.460
We're actually in
the very last--

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.030
come in a little bit.

00:08:25.030 --> 00:08:27.490
We're in the very last
time slot on Thursday,

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:29.830
so please stick around for us.

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:32.109
But we're talking about a--

00:08:32.109 --> 00:08:34.150
kind of in that same vein
of bug bounty programs,

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:36.640
we're talking about
a security issue that

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:39.820
was reported to the
Shopify bug bounty program

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:42.400
that I think is a really
interesting example

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:46.150
that, for the community in
general, we can learn from.

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:48.880
When companies share
these kind of experiences,

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.070
I think it's very
valuable to be able to dig

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:53.980
into the details of them,
be fairly open about them,

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.540
and talk about how
the attack worked,

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:58.300
how the vulnerability
was discovered,

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:00.910
how it played out, what
the possible defenses are,

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:03.370
and learn from it
as a community.

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:06.610
So Shane, maybe you can tell us
a little bit about the Shopify

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:09.865
bug bounty program and
how that got started?

00:09:09.865 --> 00:09:10.990
SHANE LAWRENCE: Sure, Greg.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:13.390
So I'm Shane from
Shopify, and the mission

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:15.190
of the security
team at Shopify is

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:17.710
to make commerce more
secure for everyone.

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:21.105
And to that end, we have a
comprehensive security posture,

00:09:21.105 --> 00:09:23.230
but we know that there are
always going to be bugs.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:25.990
So our bug bounty
program allows hundreds

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:30.290
of researchers to participate,
attempt to hack our platform,

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:32.170
and if they're able to
find vulnerabilities,

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:34.390
then there's a financial
incentive for them

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:36.880
to disclose those responsibly.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:39.370
Once they make that
responsible disclosure,

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:42.130
we will patch it as soon
as we find out about it

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:43.810
and correct the problem.

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:45.820
And as soon as we know
that we're secure again,

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:48.400
we release that out to
the public, and that way

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.059
other companies can take
advantage of this information.

00:09:51.059 --> 00:09:52.600
And through
collaboration, we can all

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:55.505
make the platforms more secure.

00:09:55.505 --> 00:09:57.555
GREG CASTLE: Great.

00:09:57.555 --> 00:09:59.750
Saad, did you want to jump
back in, or how do you--

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:00.250
yeah.

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:07.330
SAAD ALI: So do you
have any recommendations

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:11.800
for other customers who are
working with Kubernetes or GKE

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:14.260
and what they can do to
improve their security

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:15.605
on their systems?

00:10:15.605 --> 00:10:16.730
SHANE LAWRENCE: Absolutely.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:19.000
So one of the ways that
we've been able to do this

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:21.400
is by working really
closely with our partners.

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:23.770
So whenever we find
a vulnerability

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:25.540
or we suspect there
may be an opportunity

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:28.510
for a vulnerability, then we
discuss it with our partners

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:30.640
at Google, and we
get a good handle

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:34.420
on what the risk of
attack is and what would

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:36.530
happen if it were compromised.

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:38.890
And if it seems like there's
a high severity there,

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:41.230
we might be able to mitigate
it on our own platform,

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:42.970
but that doesn't
help anyone else.

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:45.490
So we can release that
information or we can

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:47.424
incorporate it
into tools we have,

00:10:47.424 --> 00:10:49.090
for instance, an open
source tool called

00:10:49.090 --> 00:10:51.340
kubeaudit that anyone can use.

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:53.290
And patches are very welcome.

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:56.590
And in that way, we're able to
improve the security posture.

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:58.690
Other people can share
their knowledge with us,

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:00.880
we can share our
knowledge with them.

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:02.350
And in addition
to that, we found

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:05.140
that one of the biggest
things that prevents people

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:08.680
from taking part in bug bounty
programs is low bounties.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:12.100
So if you're not financially
compensated for your work,

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:14.440
then researchers will
move on to someone else.

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:18.910
So Shopify is very proud that
we offer above average bug

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:19.660
bounties.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:22.990
We offer substantial
financial compensation.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:25.780
And in that way, when
researchers find problems

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:28.120
with our platform, they're
encouraged to report it,

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:29.530
and they're encouraged
to keep coming back.

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:31.988
And it really shows that we
value their time and the effort

00:11:31.988 --> 00:11:33.509
that they've put into it.

00:11:33.509 --> 00:11:35.800
SAAD ALI: Yeah, I think that
goes a long way to helping

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:37.390
secure the entire ecosystem.

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:38.487
So thank you for that.

00:11:38.487 --> 00:11:40.070
If we could switch
gears a little bit,

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:41.320
let's talk about KubeCon.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:43.324
This is not your first KubeCon?

00:11:43.324 --> 00:11:45.490
SHANE LAWRENCE: No, actually
this is the second time

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:46.600
that I've been at KubeCon.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:48.395
I was able to attend
last year in Austin.

00:11:48.395 --> 00:11:49.270
It was my first time.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:52.127
I'd been using Kubernetes for
about a month at the time,

00:11:52.127 --> 00:11:52.960
so I was pretty new.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:55.754
But I'm really excited
to be back here today.

00:11:55.754 --> 00:11:57.670
SAAD ALI: Are there any
security related talks

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:00.260
that you're excited about?

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:01.960
Of course, yours
coming up tomorrow.

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:04.445
Maybe you want to
tell folks about that?

00:12:04.445 --> 00:12:05.320
SHANE LAWRENCE: Sure.

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:07.900
So there was a
specific incident that

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:11.350
happened about six months
ago where one of our security

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:14.110
researchers found a
vulnerability in our platform,

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:15.940
and it combined a
couple of things

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:19.270
that may have seemed like they
were low risk on their own,

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:21.490
but combined, they could
have led to complete

00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:23.060
control of a cluster.

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:26.620
And so we paid our maximum
bounty of $25,000 for that,

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:29.000
and we're really proud
of the work that we did.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.180
We received the report,
and within an hour

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:34.049
and four minutes, we were able
to mitigate that vulnerability.

00:12:34.049 --> 00:12:36.340
And I think that some of the
work that we've done there

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:38.470
will be really valuable
to others who are trying

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:40.300
to secure their own platforms.

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:43.510
So I'm going to be sharing
that alongside my co-presenter,

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:44.170
Greg.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:45.687
And I'm really excited.

00:12:45.687 --> 00:12:47.770
I think that some of the
details will be valuable.

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:51.310
I also really enjoyed seeing
Maya Kaczorowski's talk

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:53.200
yesterday on
security, and I think

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:54.850
that was an incredible talk.

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:57.220
I thought one of the
most important things

00:12:57.220 --> 00:12:59.530
that she covered there
was the fact that there

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:01.420
are all of these
container escapes

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:03.490
that everyone's
really excited about.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:05.710
They're really glamorous
or sophisticated,

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:07.780
and almost no one's
using those in the wild.

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:10.070
So I was really happy to
hear her talk about that.

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:11.320
SAAD ALI: That sounds awesome.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:13.930
So if folks are interested,
please check out these talks.

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:15.680
And thank you so
much for your time.

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:15.940
SHANE LAWRENCE: Thank you.

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:17.100
SAAD ALI: Have a great
rest of your KubeCon.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:18.100
SHANE LAWRENCE: You too.

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:20.494
[MUSIC PLAYING]

